\documentclass{article}
\newcommand{\gap}[1]{|#1 - #2|}
\renewcommand{\valfun}[1]{V(#1)}
\title{Inventory Replenishment with Demand Learning}
\author{R. Caceres and M. Abiodun}
\date{May 2026}
\begin{document}
\maketitle
\section{Introduction}
Retailers replenish stock under demand that drifts with season and trend.
We study replenishment rules that learn the demand rate online
and bound the cost of being wrong.

\section{Model}
Each period opens with on-hand stock $s$ and a demand draw.
The one-period optimality gap of a candidate rule is written
$\gap{c}{h}$ for holding cost $h$ and underage cost $c$.
The value function $\valfun{s}$ aggregates discounted cost
over the planning horizon.

\section{Learning Rule}
The demand rate is updated by Bayesian tracking,
and the order-up-to level follows the posterior quantile.
The rule needs only the running mean and variance of demand.

\section{Results}
On synthetic series the learned rule closes most of the gap
to the clairvoyant benchmark within one selling season.
Performance degrades gracefully when the demand drift is misspecified.

\section{Conclusion}
Demand learning is a cheap upgrade to classical replenishment,
and its benefit is largest where seasons turn quickly.
\end{document}

